<p>Some of those (<abbr title="The Black Sword Hack">BSH</abbr>
, <cite>Ironsworn: Starforged</cite>
, <cite>Mythic Bastionland</cite>
, and <abbr title="The One Ring">TOR</abbr>
) were
solo games, which means I might return to those games if/when the mood strikes.</p>
<p>I recall when I last ran a game of <cite><a href="https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/108028/Dungeon-World?affiliate_id=318171">Dungeon World</a></cite> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Dungeon World”" title="Other site-wide references of “Dungeon World”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-DUNGEON-WORLD">&#128214;</a></small>
, I had derisive thoughts that
“This game is really about using a bunch of parlor tricks.”  Yet I didn’t write
about that.  Instead I wrote about this in <a href="https://takeonrules.com/2017/02/26/randomness-i-forgot-that-i-needed-you/">Randomness, I Forgot that I Needed
You</a>.</p>
<h2 id="parlor-tricks-or-entertaining-a-group-of-people">Parlor Tricks or Entertaining a Group of People</h2>
<p>What did I mean by parlor tricks?  Namely that you ask leading questions to
build towards agenda.  Which in real life is a bad idea, and hence my derision.
But at a game table parlor tricks is what we’ve signed up for.</p>
<p>Regarding my other issues with <cite>Dungeon World</cite>
, there were two:</p>
<ul>
<li>the initial bonds</li>
<li>the lack of randomness</li>
</ul>
<p>Given Stonetop’s deep interweaving of setting and rules, the initial character
relations are far more natural feeling.  They appear later in the character
generation process, and naturally flow from previous information.</p>
<p>And the lack of randomness?  Stonetop provides character facing questions that
give them narrative agency, which is a bit like adding randomness.</p>
<h2 id="randomness-and-cognitive-load">Randomness and Cognitive Load</h2>
<p>A goal of the randomness is to introduce and then incorporate the unexpected
into the fiction; that is to be surprised by the direction the story takes.  I
also think more important still is to callback to that unexpected.</p>
<p>Randomness, via reaction rolls, encounter tables, and morale are available
concepts to incorporate.  One could even make a random move table to consult.</p>
<p>Yet randomness and its incorporation comes with a cognitive toll.  Having run
one session of Stonetop, I am now remembering that games with the equivalent of
<em>Discern Reality</em> and <em>Spout Lore</em> introduce significant cognitive load.</p>
<p>I recently talked with a fellow forever <span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamemaster">Game Master</a></span> (<abbr title="Game Master">GM</abbr> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Game Master”" title="Other site-wide references of “Game Master”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-GM">&#128214;</a></small>)
 friend and asked if he too felt an
almost compulsion to fill “dead air” with conversation.<small class="side-container">
  <span class="side-label"><span class="hidden">(</span>Sidenote<span class="hidden">:</span></span>
  <span class="side" role="note"> I’m wondering if there are other forever game facilitators who feel this
same compulsion?<span class="hidden">)</span></span>
</small>
 I explained that as
an often <abbr title="Game Master">GM</abbr>
 since <time datetime="1988" title="1988">1988</time>, I had conditioned myself that in a group setting, when
there was silence, I should talk.  After all, the <abbr title="Game Master">GM</abbr>
 is the conduit for the
group to interact with the game.</p>
<p>Which is gets to the load I feel from moves like <em>Discern Reality</em> and <em>Spout Lore</em>;
I have a compulsion to answer quickly.  And sometimes, the players make those
moves in unexpected places, and I have little prep work to help answer that.  So
I feel an almost physiological discomfort of the silence and expectation of
filling that silence.</p>
<h2 id="carrying-the-cognitive-load">Carrying the Cognitive Load</h2>
<p>I am favoring an analogue approach for campaign management.</p>
<p>Between sessions, I review the last session, look for threats and ask questions
that I’m curious about.  This is where I explore the situation.  I write, I
doodle, I let my pencil wander on the page.  As much as a map, this is where our
localized quantum state of Stonetop takes shape.<small class="side-container">
  <span class="side-label"><span class="hidden">(</span>Sidenote<span class="hidden">:</span></span>
  <span class="side" role="note"> It is during the session at the table where our localized actual Stonetop takes shape.<span class="hidden">)</span></span>
</small>
</p>
<p>I also have an old army green metal case for note cards that contains threats
and <span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-player_character">Non-Player Characters</a></span> (<abbr title="Non-Player Characters">NPCs</abbr> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Non-Player Characters”" title="Other site-wide references of “Non-Player Characters”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-NPC">&#128214;</a></small>)
.  During preparation, I review each of those threats and make any notes
or updates on the note card or in my notebook.</p>
<p>During play, I have a large dot notebook open to a two page spread.  The left
page is primarily for prep, the right page is for notes.</p>
<p>On the left page, I mount a two column print out, with margins removed, of my
preparation.  This leaves a narrow column on the right of that left page (11
columns wide) and a narrow footer (roughly 9 rows for writing).</p>
<p>I’ve committed to transcribing relevant information onto these pages.  For
example, for session 2, I hand-wrote the weather table for early spring.  That
way as they travel, I can introduce weather accordingly.</p>
<p>The act of “formalizing” my prep, by printing out a page, serves two purposes.
First, it is analogous to writing up a paper based on research notes.  Second,
the act of mounting the print out to a page in the notebook signifies a
completion of preparation.</p>
<p>As stated in <a href="https://takeonrules.com/2026/07/10/session-zero-of-stonetop/">Session Zero of Stonetop</a>, one goal is to have a durable artifact
for this game.  Another goal is that in favoring handwriting, and according to
research, I’m favoring conditions for better synthesis of the game.  That is,
I’ll hopefully have more in my head, at the expense of not having it quickly
machine searchable.</p>
<h2 id="remembering-the-good">Remembering the Good</h2>
<p>My job, software engineer, is to look around corners for the edge cases; where
things break or become fragile.  And this work has created default pessimistic
tendencies in my approaches.  Which is to say, that this post addressed the
negative but did not yet account for the positive.</p>
<p>I love that Stonetop and <abbr title="Powered by the Apocalypse">PbtA</abbr>
 games focus on asking questions, which is not to
say that other <span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Role-playing_game">Role Playing Games</a></span> (<abbr title="Role Playing Games">RPGs</abbr> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Role Playing Games”" title="Other site-wide references of “Role Playing Games”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-RPG">&#128214;</a></small>)
 don’t, but instead that <abbr title="Powered by the Apocalypse">PbtA</abbr>
’s focus on conversation and
fiction triggerign moves, creates, for me, immersive games.  Ones where I
suspect the shared vision of the game tends towards greater alignment.</p>
<p>And Stonetop, with its setting book, doubles down on the questions; equipping
the game facilitator with a plethora of questions that help meld the shared
vision of the players with the designer’s vision.</p>
<p>There is a magic in play with the questions provided within the books—focusing
on developing a care and concern for the community of Stonetop.  Which is an
activity that each game group playing Stonetop will engage in.  And thus
creating a myriad of iron age villages that each group cares about and which
carries similarities and differences with each other.</p>
<p>Providing a common touchstone of comparison and story telling between game
groups.</p>
<p>Another aspect I love about <abbr title="Powered by the Apocalypse">PbtA</abbr>
 games is how “decisive” they can be; I think of
their contrast with mid-level d20 combat slogs.  I don’t want to spend 45
minutes, or more, on a single combat.</p>
<p>I also love how the moves of <abbr title="Powered by the Apocalypse">PbtA</abbr>
 games tend to create a myriad of sub-systems
that have the same interface at the table; namely the fiction triggers the move
and de-prioritizes one approach over another.  That is, there is no <em>roll for
initiative</em> signifying that we start playing a different kind of game.</p>
<p>And then there is the baseline resolution system, with it’s “negotiated
success.”  I love systems that move away from the binary resolution towards a
more nuanced resolution.  Our world is not binary, why should our games resolve
as such?</p>
<h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
<p>For now, I’ll hold off on randomness, as I get back to running a face to face
<abbr title="Powered by the Apocalypse">PbtA</abbr>
.  What I know to be different is that Stonetop provides a rich setting that
surprises me as I prepare my game.</p>
<p>I’m also a different person than when I last ran games.  I have worked to become
a better active listener.  I have accumulated quite a bit more tips and tricks
of play; in part thanks to the burgeoning <abbr title="Role Playing Game">RPG</abbr>
 podcast culture of actual play and
discussions.</p>
<p>I’ve listened to quite a few actual play podcasts and thought, “Oh, as the <abbr title="Game Master">GM</abbr>
 I
wouldn’t have responded that way but instead this way.”</p>
<p>I’ve also run several different games and flexed my solo-play skills, something
that has helped me better respond to the unexpected.  In part, because I have no
preparation for any of those solo games, and instead rely on oracles and other
supporting materials for solo play.</p>
<p>Which reminds me that I should take inventory of those solo games, the tools
they provide, and look at what I might be missing from my assumptive Stonetop
toolkit.</p>

